export const DE_C2_S01_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "de-das-komma": [
    `For thirty-one years Marie Holzapfel had edited Konrad Wendt's novels, and for thirty-one years they had argued about commas, or, as she herself liked to put it, about "Kommata". The publishing house, a venerable and chronically hard-up firm in a side street in Göttingen, had long grown used to the noises that occasionally came from her room on the second floor, noises that in other lines of business would have been put down to a marital row. Wendt wrote the way other people breathe: hastily, irregularly, with long pauses in places where none belonged. Marie, by contrast, believed in the official spelling rules the way others believe in the resurrection, only with better evidence.`,
    `Now his last novel lay before her. That it would be the last he had told her on the phone, with the same offhand finality with which he usually cancelled lunches: "I'm eighty-three, Marie. The doctors are still polite, but no longer optimistic." The manuscript was called "Spätlese" (Late Harvest), and it was, she had to admit to herself after the third reading, his best book: the story of an old hatter from Hann. Münden who had loved a woman called Clara all his life without ever telling her, because every time the moment came, a subordinate clause got in the way.`,
    `The quarrel flared up, how could it have been otherwise, over the very last sentence. On page 412, added afterwards in pencil, it said: "I do not love you, Clara." Marie read it four times, and each time it seemed more wrong to her. After four hundred pages of unspoken tenderness this book could not end with a rejection; that would not have been an ending but a blow. She put a thin question mark in the margin and below it, in her small, incorruptible handwriting: "Breaks with the whole characterisation. Intention or oversight? Please check. M. H."`,
    `Two weeks later the manuscript came back, by post, because Wendt considered emails a form of rudeness. Marie leafed through it and couldn't believe her eyes. He had accepted all her corrections, every single one, even the semicolons, which he had fought for three decades like an occupying power. Only on page 412 had he changed something. The comma had moved by a single word: "I love you, not Clara." Below it, in a hand that now trembled more than his style: "Now it's right."`,
    `First she read the sentence as an editor. Grammatically it was flawless, as storytelling an outrage: whom was the hatter supposed to love, if not Clara? There was no other woman in the whole novel, not even a minor character who could come into question. Then she read it a second time, more slowly, and suddenly it was as if someone had moved the furniture in a familiar room. Clara was invented. The readers would only come later, in spring, with reviews and reading glasses. But before all of them, for thirty-one years, there had only ever been one person who read every one of Konrad Wendt's sentences first.`,
    `She called him, something she otherwise never did without first making a list. "That's a comma mistake, Konrad." "No," he said, and she could hear that he was smiling. "It's the first sentence in thirty-one years in which I've put the comma in the right place. You taught me, after all." For a while she heard only his breathing, hasty, irregular, with pauses in places where none belonged. "And now?" she asked at last. "Now you're the editor," he said. "You decide where it goes."`,
    `"Spätlese" came out in March, six weeks after the funeral. More was written about the comma on the last page than about the rest of the book. A major arts section interpreted it as a postmodern gesture, a tear in the fiction through which the author waved one last time; a literary scholar from Tübingen took it for a misprint and wrote the publisher an indignant letter. The press office replied to every enquiry that the editor responsible did not wish to comment.`,
    `But in Marie's own copy, on page 412, written in pencil in the margin, there is a single word. She has written it under other people's sentences several thousand times in her working life, and only this one time does she mean it exactly as it stands: "Agreed."`,
  ],
  "de-blindgaenger": [
    `The unexploded bomb had lain for eighty-one years seven metres down beneath a potato field on the edge of Oranienburg, and for eighty-one years nothing, strictly speaking, had been ticking inside it. That was the problem. In the spring of 1945 thousands of bombs with chemical long-delay fuses had been dropped on the town, meant to detonate hours or days after impact, and some of them had changed their minds. An ampoule of acetone that hadn't broken; a celluloid disc that hadn't dissolved; a firing pin that had been waiting ever since. Nobody could say for what.`,
    `Mirko Hentschel of the Brandenburg state bomb disposal service had defused a hundred and forty bombs and blown up seven in twenty-nine years of service. He was a heavy, calm man with hands like loaves of bread that nobody would have believed capable of what they could do, and he had the habit of talking to the bombs, quietly, in a tone his wife called his dentist voice. He had looked at this one in the morning, and he had not talked to it. The fuse was deformed, the pin probably cocked. It would have to be blown up on the spot. Exclusion zone one thousand metres, four thousand people, a care home, two nurseries. And a Ms Kunze, Edith, 14 Akazienweg, who refused to leave.`,
    `The police had rung twice, the public order office once, and then pushed a note through the letterbox mentioning, in careful officialese, the possibility of enforcing the evacuation order by compulsion. Ms Kunze had turned the note over and written on the back: "I was born here, and I'm not leaving because of a bomb that can't make up its mind." At half past one, an hour before the planned detonation, Mirko Hentschel himself stood at her door. He had left his protective vest in the car, because nobody is convincing in a protective vest.`,
    `She opened at once, as if she had been standing behind the door. A small woman, her hair freshly done as if she were expecting visitors, and evidently she was: on the kitchen table stood two cups and a plate of crumb cake. "You're the blasting man," she said. "Sit down. You've still got an hour." He sat down, because you don't contradict an eighty-one-year-old with crumb cake, and because in twenty-nine years he had learned that every bomb has its own way of being defused.`,
    `"I was born on the fifteenth of March, forty-five," she said as she poured the coffee. "In the cellar, under this house. My mother told me the walls were shaking upstairs and the midwife started the Lord's Prayer from the beginning at every impact, so that she never got as far as our daily bread. Afterwards there were things like that in the ground everywhere here. We played on them as children without knowing it. For half my life something has gone off somewhere in this town, sometimes a field, sometimes a garage, once a digger along with its driver." She pushed the cake towards him. "Do you know what I am, Mr Hentschel? I'm one of those duds too. Dropped in a hail of bombs, didn't go off, eighty-one years in the ground, and nobody knows what I'm actually still waiting for."`,
    `He could have explained to her now why long-delay fuses don't become more harmless with age but more temperamental; that the celluloid disc grows brittle and the spring tired, and that nobody can predict which tremor will be the last. Instead he ate a piece of cake and said: "Then this is what we'll do. You come along, but not to the sports hall with the others. You come with me to the command post. You watch." Ms Kunze looked at him for a long time. "And if I'd rather stay here?" "Then I'll stay too," he said, "and you can explain to my wife why."`,
    `At two thirty-two Edith Kunze stood in a borrowed helmet that slipped down over her eyebrows, behind a wall of sandbags eight hundred metres from the field, holding on to Hentschel's sleeve. He spoke into the radio, counted backwards, and at zero the earth rose in the distance, as if someone had clenched a fist under a tablecloth. Only then came the bang, a dull, long-rolling blow that went through the soles of their shoes into their stomachs. A brown fountain stood over the fields for a second and collapsed. Birds flew up. Somewhere a dog barked and stopped again at once, as if the whole thing embarrassed it.`,
    `Ms Kunze took off the helmet. She didn't cry, as he had feared; she laughed, briefly and brightly, like a girl. "Eighty-one years," she said, "and that's the first bang in my life I wasn't afraid of." On the way back in the official car she asked whether he would come again; there were supposedly still around three hundred of them in the town. Mirko Hentschel promised. Up to his retirement he defused another nineteen bombs and blew up three, and to every detonation, it says so nowhere in the files, but everyone in the service knows it, he brought a second helmet.`,
  ],
  "de-die-letzte-stimme": [
    `On the night after the cantor had died, the house by the St. Thomas School smelled of tallow and vinegar. The windows stood open, because it was July and because the women believed the soul had to find a way out, and from outside came the cursing of the carters, who cared nothing about the death of an old musician. Gottlob Eckhardt, nineteen years old, a boarding pupil of the St. Thomas School and for two years one of the household's copyists, sat in the writing room under the roof and waited for someone to tell him what was to be done now.`,
    `In the end it was the widow herself who came up the stairs with a bundle of paper. Anna Magdalena had been crying, but she spoke as calmly as if it were about a cantata for next Sunday. Her stepson, she said, wished to hand over to the engraver the great work of fugues on which his father had worked to the very end, and for that a clean copy was needed. The last fugue was not finished. "He dictated it when he could no longer see," she said, "and then he stopped." She laid the sheets on the desk, touched them briefly with two fingers, the way one strokes the forehead of someone sleeping, and went back down.`,
    `Gottlob drew the staves with the rastrum, five lines at once, as he had done a thousand times, and began to copy. He knew the cantor's handwriting better than his own: its impatience, the upward-fleeing stems of the quavers, the ink blots the old man would turn into noteheads with a wipe of his finger. But in the last months, after the operations by the English eye surgeon who had passed through the city with a large retinue and left behind more bills than cures, this hand had become strange: crooked, groping, often completed by another hand. Gottlob copied, bar by bar, and the tallow candle burned down.`,
    `At about two in the morning he came to the place. The cantor had layered three themes on top of one another, and the third, only just introduced, consisted of four notes: B flat, A, C, B natural, which in German are called B, A, C, H. Gottlob hummed them quietly to himself and felt the hairs on the back of his neck stand up. The man had written his own name into the music, not as a signature beneath the work but right in the middle of it, as one voice among voices. And some forty bars later, just after all three themes had sounded together for the first time, everything broke off. No final chord, no cadence, not even a bar line. The middle voice hung in the air like a sentence someone has interrupted.`,
    `He knew how it had to go on. That was the monstrous thing. He had studied counterpoint with the cantor for three years, with slaps and with praise, which was rarer and hurt more, and the logic of this fugue lay before him like a road you walk in the dark because you have walked it a hundred times by day. Twenty, perhaps thirty bars, then the stretto of all four themes, then the end. No one would notice. The engraver would get a finished work, the son a worthy conclusion, the world a fugue without a wound. He dipped his quill.`,
    `For a long time he held it over the paper. A drop of ink gathered at the tip, grew heavy, fell and left a blot on the empty staff, exactly where the next bar should have begun. Gottlob looked at it. The old man would have turned it into a notehead with a single wipe and written on. But the old man was dead, and what was missing here was not missing through carelessness. Whoever finished this fugue, Gottlob thought, would not be lying about the music, but about death. The fugue he could have completed; the man who had begun it, not.`,
    `He wiped his quill and copied only the last bar, just as it stood, broken, with the hanging middle voice. He left the rest of the page empty, five lines beneath five lines, white to the edge. When the widow came up in the morning, she found him asleep over the desk, his head on his arm. She took the copy, looked at the empty page for a long time and asked nothing. Later it was said that she had taken the blot on the first empty bar for a tear.`,
    `Gottlob Eckhardt became an organist in a small town in the Ore Mountains, married, fathered seven children and in his long life copied thousands of parts, sopranos and basses, violins and oboes, for weddings, funerals and ordinary Sundays. He seldom spoke of his time in Leipzig. Only once, when a young pupil asked him which part had been the hardest he had ever copied, is he said to have thought for a long time and then answered: "The one I did not write."`,
  ],
  "de-stimmt": [
    `Heinrich Ahlers had been tuning the pianos of Flensburg and the surrounding area since 1966, and in all those years less had changed in his craft than in his face. The same worn leather bag, the same tuning hammer with its wooden handle gripped black, the same tuning fork that he struck against his knee and held to his ear, although a device had long been on the market that knew concert pitch more precisely than he did. Outside, the town had changed its face several times; shipyards had closed, harbour warehouses had been converted into lofts, pubs turned into cafés. Inside, in the living rooms, the pianos stood unmoved against their walls and went out of tune.`,
    `The young woman who called him in October said "Moin" in the tone of a Flensburg girl who studies in Hamburg and asked whether he still made house calls. "It's my grandmother's piano, in Glücksburg. She said that if anyone was to tune it, it should be you. Only you. She died in September." The next morning Heinrich stood in a living room overlooking the fjord, in front of a black upright piano from the fifties, solid, unspectacular, an instrument for a family that valued order and Christmas carols.`,
    `He opened the lid, unscrewed the front panel, and there it was, the thing laypeople never get to see: the inside of the case, written on like the wall of a prison cell. Piano tuners sign their work, initials and a date, pencilled onto the wood, a silent bookkeeping across decades. A colleague from Kiel in 1979, one from Schleswig in 1991, an illegible flourish from 2004. And right at the bottom of the frame, smaller than all the others, the oldest signature: H. A. 4. 68. Next to it, so faint that it could also be taken for the grain of the wood, two words he had written fifty-eight years ago with a trembling hand: I'm waiting.`,
    `In the spring of 1968 Wiebke Lorenzen had been twenty-one and had typed payrolls in the office of the shipyard. He had tuned her parents' piano three times that spring, twice without any reason at all, and each time she had walked him to the garden gate and never asked why a piano could go out of tune so often. He had said little that spring, far too little, and in the summer she had married a ship's engineer from Kiel who talked more. The two words inside the case were the bravest thing Heinrich Ahlers had done in his life, and no one had ever read them.`,
    `Then he saw the other letters, directly beneath. Not a tuner's hand: too big, pressed too hard, uneven, as if someone had worked in half-darkness, half lying down, with their head inside the instrument. Me too. W. Heinrich took off his glasses because they had fogged up. When had she unscrewed the front panel, alone, with a screwdriver from the kitchen drawer, and found what she had perhaps always suspected? And why hadn't she simply got on the bus, twenty minutes, no more? He knew the answer, of course. A husband, three children, a house on the fjord. "Me too" was exactly as much as one could say without breaking anything.`,
    `"Is it very out of tune?" the young woman asked from the doorway. Heinrich cleared his throat. "A piano that nobody plays doesn't go out of tune," he said. "It just forgets. A string has broken, in the treble. That's the most common thing." "Can it be repaired?" "Everything can be repaired," he said, "that isn't made of flesh." She looked at him, and only now did he notice that she had Wiebke's eyes, that grey waiting that forced you to keep talking. "Do you know what's lovely about my job?" he asked. "At the end you're allowed to say: it's right, it's in tune. How many things in life can you say that about?"`,
    `That day he took three hours, twice as long as necessary. He fitted the new string, turned each tuning pin by fractions of a degree, listened for the beats until they disappeared, and tuned the instrument more precisely than an upright of this kind had ever deserved. When Jule Hansen wanted to pay him, he shook his head. "That's already been paid for," he said, "a long time ago." At the garden gate, which was the same as back then, she said she would probably not sell the piano after all.`,
    `Before he had screwed the front panel back on, he had taken the pencil and, as is proper, signed his work, beneath all the others, beneath the "I'm waiting" and beneath the "Me too": H. A. 10. 26. And after a brief hesitation, with a hand that was steadier than fifty-eight years before, one more word: "Stimmt", in tune, and true.`,
  ],
  "de-die-woerterboerse": [
    `In the beginning was the Word, and in the year 2087 it belonged to the Deutsche Wörterbörse AG, the German Word Exchange, headquartered at Spittelmarkt in Berlin. At least that is what it said, slightly modified, above the entrance, in brass letters for whose use the exchange paid licence fees to itself. Since the language rights reform of 2061 every word of the German language had been a tradable asset. Whoever said it or wrote it paid a fraction of a cent to its respective owner through the implant behind the ear. Only a few hundred words were in the public domain: articles, pronouns, numbers, yes, no, good, also and, for reasons of social policy, bread. All other prices fluctuated with demand. In winter "mulled wine" rose, during election campaigns "future", during international matches everything that rhymed with "ref".`,
    `Ronja Albrecht, twenty-eight, traded in emotional assets, the most volatile division of the firm. She had a feel for moods, in the literal as well as the figurative sense: she knew that "longing" picked up in November, that "security" shot up after every crisis and that "hope" was chronically overvalued. In private she spoke, like most people in the business, sparingly. With her boyfriend Mats, who as a bicycle courier earned hardly more than the public-domain words cost, that is, nothing, she communicated at home in that terse German the arts pages called the new Low German: "Good?" "Good." "Bread?" "Yes." It was enough. Mostly.`,
    `On the eve of Valentine's Day Ronja got the kind of tip you only get once in a lifetime. The consortium that held "Liebe", love, the most expensive noun in the German language, wanted to give it to the public in a generous gesture, to clear itself of a threatening antitrust suit. The next day at twelve noon "love", together with its whole word family, would enter the public domain. Until then nobody knew except the board, two lawyers and, through an indiscretion better not traced back, Ronja. She short-sold whatever she could get: "love", "to love", "darling", even the old-fashioned "dearest", which only poets and marriage swindlers still bought.`,
    `In the evening the price stood at an all-time high. Half of Berlin wanted to say "I love you" the next morning, and the other half at least wanted to hear it. Ronja sat on the sofa with her terminal and did her sums. When the news came tomorrow at noon, she would earn enough to speak in full subordinate clauses for a year. Perhaps even in the subjunctive, whose boom had continued unbroken for years because only the upper class could still afford it. She imagined saying to Mats: "We could go away, if you wanted." Six words, two of them in the subjunctive. A fortune.`,
    `Mats came home late, sweaty, with a bunch of tulips wrapped in paper that had clearly cost more than it should have. He sat down beside her, took her hand and looked at her with a seriousness she did not know in him. Then he said it. Three words, clearly, unhurriedly, at the peak price. Ronja heard the soft click in his implant confirming the debit, and she knew without checking how much it was: roughly three months' rent. He must have been saving since the summer. Every evening "Good?" "Good.", so that on this one evening he wouldn't have to skimp.`,
    `On the terminal beside her the price twitched upwards by one tiny blip, and Ronja understood with a clarity that almost took her breath away that this blip was Mats. That she had bet against exactly this sentence. That tomorrow at twelve she would profit from it, from his summer, from every swallowed word. It was, she thought, the most expensive sentence ever spoken in this flat, and she was the only one in the room who knew that by tomorrow noon it would cost nothing. From tomorrow on everyone would say it, carelessly, at the supermarket checkout, in the stairwell, to dogs. Today it had cost Mats everything.`,
    `She could have said many things to him now. That he was an idiot. That he should have waited until tomorrow. That she could have, subjunctive, treated him for a year out of her profit. Instead she reached for the terminal and closed all her positions, at a loss, and the click of her own implant sounded to her at that moment like the most beautiful sound in the world. Then she turned to him. She chose her answer with the care of a woman who deals in words for a living, and she chose the cheapest there were, three public-domain words that cost not a single cent and that she had never meant more seriously in her life: "I you too", that is, I love you too.`,
  ],
};

